    "summary": "Smoking Other One marathon, tight China>Rider segue and stellar jams",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a smoking The Other One suite with multiple reprises, a seamless China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider transition and high‑energy Truckin' jam. Jerry’s guitar shines on It Hurts Me Too and the extended improvisations, while Keith adds warm organ textures throughout. Near‑perfect soundboard recordings capture every nuance.",
    "review": "May 3, 1972 at the Olympia Theater finds the Dead in top form, unleashing a marathon of high‑octane improvisation that still feels fresh today. The first set builds momentum with a flawless China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider segue and a fiery It Hurts Me Too, but it’s the second‑set monster that steals the show: Truckin' erupts into an unbroken jam that folds into The Other One, then rolls through Drums, another reprise of The Other One, Bobby McGee and finally Wharf Rat. Jerry Garcia’s wah‑wah tone and double‑bends drive the suite, Phil Lesh’s “bombs” of bass anchor the chaos, and Bill Kreutzmann’s rapid tom rolls keep everything locked in. Keith Godchaux’s organ adds lush depth on Jack Straw and He's Gone, while Bob Weir provides solid rhythm work and a standout vocal on Sugar Magnolia. The performance is captured with near‑perfect soundboard clarity, making this one of the most compelling documents of the Europe ’72 tour.",
